# Frame and overlays: a Page overlay repeats a header and a footer on every page, in the margin of the page.
Document (dev.stxt.pdf):
	Language: en
	Title: Frame and overlays · Page overlay
	Page:
		Paper: A5
		Margin: 60 40 60 40
	Styles:
		For element: Page overlay
			Typography:
				Size: 8
				Line height: 10
				Color: muted
	Page overlay:
		Offset: -36 0 auto 0
		Box:
			Padding: 0 0 4 0
			Border: 0 0 0.5 0
			Border color: rule
		Text: Reference of the layout language · Positioned elements
	Page overlay:
		Offset: auto 0 -36 0
		Text: Page {page} of {pages}
			Typography:
				Align: right
	Section:
		Text: Frame and overlays
			Style: topic
		Heading 1:
			Text: Page overlay
		Paragraph: A `Page overlay` is placed like an `Overlay`, against the page area, and painted on every page. It takes no space, so the `Margin` of the `Page` leaves room for it, and a negative `Offset` takes it into that margin. Its text can write `{{page}}` and `{{pages}}`.
			Style: intro
		Paragraph: The header of this document is a Page overlay with Offset: -36 0 auto 0, 36 points above the page area, with a bottom border. The footer has Offset: auto 0 -36 0 and the text Page {{page}} of {{pages}}, aligned to the right.
		Paragraph: Both are children of the Document, so they appear in every section. A Page overlay inside a Section appears only on the pages of that section.
		Paragraph: The content of a Page overlay is decoration of the page: a screen reader does not read it and it cannot hold headings.
			Break:
				After: page
		Paragraph: The second page carries the same header and footer, with its own number.
			Break:
				After: page
		Paragraph: And so does the third.
